# Shape- and size-dependent catalysis activities of iron-terephthalic acid metal-organic frameworks

Yali Liu1, Pengfei Gao2, Chengzhi Huang2 & Yuanfang Li1
1Key Laboratory of Luminescent and Real-Time Analytical Chemistry (Southwest University), Ministry of Education; College of Chemistry and Chemical Engineering, Southwest University, Chongqing 400715, China 2College of Pharmaceutical Science, Southwest University, Chongqing 400716, China
Precise control of the size and morphology of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) presents an important direction for extending these inorganic-organic materials to many more advanced applications. However, because of the limit of the crystal-growth rule and mechanism, good-control of the size and morphology of MOFs remains challenging. In this contribution, an iron-terephthalic acid metal-organic framework with different shapes (octahedron, spindle and bipyramidal hexagonal) were easily and reproducibly synthesized via a solvothermal method. Sodium acetate and glycerol were used as modulators. Mechanism studies showed that the crystal nucleation rate and orientational growth both play important roles in determining the final shape of the MOFs. Further investigations showed that the as prepared MOFs exhibit shape-dependent catalytic activities, which mean that MOFs can be designed to perform different catalytic functions. This investigation not only provides an effective guideline for the precise control of the size and morphology of metal-organic frameworks, but also extends MOFs to much more advanced applications in terms of catalyst chemistry.
controllable synthesis, metal-organic frameworks, catalytic activity
## 1  Introduction
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a new class of porous inorganic-organic materials [1–4]. Their alterable framework topologies, tunable pore sizes, and high surface areas have attracted much attention for their promising applications related to gas storage and separation [5], chemical sensing [6–8], drug delivery [9,10], catalysis [11], sample collection [12] and extraction [13–15] and chromatographic separation [16,17]. Previous researches seems to have focused mainly on the design, synthesis and applications of novel MOFs, by introducing different metal ions and organic ligands to improve their properties and extend their applications [18,19]. This may be because the applications of MOFs were dependent not only on their chemical composition, but also on their microstructure, morphology and size [20]. Controlling the size and shape of MOFs are significant for maximizing their performances in various advanced applications.
Several strategies have been developed for the controllable synthesis of MOFs by adding varieties of modulators to mediate the coordination and growth of the nucleation, which in fact depends on the acidity, ion-strength, or polarity of the reaction medium. For example, Kitagawa et al. [21] reported the morphology-controlled synthesis of $[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}]n$ octahedrons, cuboctahedrons, and cubic microcrystals by the addition of lauric acid as coordination modulator. Zhang et al. [22] described a simple and straightforward method for the synthesis of $\mathrm{Dy(BTC)(H_{2}O)}$ nanocrystals using sodium carboxylates as the capping agent. Guo et al. [23] presented a simple solvothermal method to fabricate $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-53(Al) crystals with controllable size and morphology by altering the ratio of water in the DMF-water mixed solvent. Yang et al. [24] employed mixed ligand to control the size and morphology of ZIF-78. Among these methods, the crystal nucleation and growth mechanism were not fully understood. Consequently, understanding the growth mechanisms of metal-organic frameworks, which is critical to the development of new approaches to the controllable synthesis of MOFs, is highly desirable and requires further study.
We developed a facile and general route for the controllable synthesis of iron-based metal-organic frameworks using $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and terephthalic acid as the reactants. The iron-based MOFs with different shapes including octahedron, spindle, and bipyramidal hexagon were easily and reproducibly obtained by adjusting the solvents or deprotonation agent, the as-prepared iron-based MOFs exhibit shape- and size- dependent peroxidase-like activity (Scheme 1). Specifically, when glycerol instead of sodeium acetate was introduced into the reactants, the bipyramidal hexagonal shape could be easily obtained. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first controllable synthesis of MOFs with glycerol as a modulator. This work provides an effective guideline for the precise control of the size and shape of MOFs for various advanced applications.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Materials
Terephthalic acid $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC)}$ , $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -azino-bis(3-ethylbenzothiazoline-6-sulfonic acid) diammonium salt (ABTS) and $_{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetramethylbenzidine (TMB) were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich (USA). $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , acetic acid, sodium acetate, sodium tetraborate, ethanol, glycerol, and glycol were obtained from Chongqing Pharmaceutical Co., Ltd. (China) and Keyi Assay Glass Branch (Chongqing,
China). All reagents were used without further purification, and all solutions were prepared using deionized water. All glassware was cleaned with aqua regia solution $\mathrm{(HCl{:}H N O_{3}}$ , 3:1) and subsequently rinsed with a copious amounts of Mili-Q purified water.
## 2.2  Preparation of standard solution
In a typical synthesis, $0.115\mathrm{~g~}$ 1 $\left\langle0.692\mathrm{mmol}\right\rangle$ of terephthalic acid and $0.187\ \mathrm{g}$ $(0.692\ \mathrm{mmol})$ of $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ were dissolved in $15~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF. The mixture was defined as the standard solution for further adjustment of expected shape and size of the iron-based MOFs, which were very stable. No precipitation was observed after this solution was kept at room temperature for as long as two weeks.
## 2.3  Synthesis of octahedron
The standard synthesis solution was placed in an oil bath at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize4h}$ , during which the bright yellow solution changed to yellow milky precipitation. After being cooled to room temperature, the precipitation was isolated by centrifugation and washed with DMF and ethanol to remove excess reactants. Finally, the octahedron particles were dried in a vacuum oven at $35~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . By analyzing the SEM images of octahedron MOFs after 6 months, the obtained particles in the form of solid powder were found to be stable in organic solvents and water.
## 2.4  Synthesis of spindle
To the standard solution, 0.2–1.5 equiv. of sodium acetate was added. A dark red milky colloidal solution formed immediately. The pH value of the solution was measured, after which this solution was placed in an oil bath at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $4\mathrm{h}$ to allow crystal growth. After crystallization, the $\mathrm{\pH}$ value of the solution was measured again, the washing and drying procedures were the same as for the octahedron. The resulting particles in the form of solid powder, were also found to be stable in organic solvents and water. To further investigate the important role of sodium acetate in controlling the size of MOFs, sodium tetraborate, which has a function similar to sodium acetate, was used for comparison. The detailed experimental process is shown in Supporting Information online.
## 2.5  Synthesis of bipyramidal hexagonal
The standard solution was first placed in an oil bath at $90^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . After $45~\mathrm{{min}}$ of nucleation, $1~\mathrm{mL}$ glycerol was added, and then the mixed solution was left to react for $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ for crystallization. The processes for sample purification and collection were the same as described in the synthesis of the octahedron. The resulting particles were found to be stable in organic solvents and in the form of solid powder. In order to understand the effect of glycerol in the crystallization process, glycol was used to replace glycerol as the control reagent. The detailed experimental process is shown in Supporting Information online.
## 2.6  Peroxidase-like activity measurements
Iron-terephthalic acid MOFs with different shapes were first dispersed in ethanol. Then the catalytic properties of different shapes were measured by using $0.04~\mathrm{mg/mL}$ MOFs, 1 mmol/L $^{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime},}$ -tetramethylbenzidine (TMB), and 100 $\upmu\mathrm{mol}/\mathrm{L}\ \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in NaAc buffer solution $\mathrm{(pH~4,~45~^{\circ}C)}$ . The oxidation of the TMB substrate to the corresponding oxidized product was directly monitored at $650~\mathrm{nm}$ by observing the absorption spectra of the solution.
## 2.7 Characterization
Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images were obtained on a Tecnai G2 F20 S-TWIN (FEI Company, USA). Scanning electron microscope (SEM) images were taken on an S-4800 scanning electron microscope (Hitachi, Japan). Powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were collected on an XD-3 X-ray diffractometer (Purkinje, China) with $\mathrm{Cu}\ K\upalpha$ radiation $(\lambda{=}1.5406\mathrm{~}\mathring{\mathrm{A}})$ in the range of $2{-}20\theta$ at a scan rate of $2.0^{\circ}/\mathrm{min}$ . Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectra were recorded on FITI-8400 (Shimadzu, Japan) in the range of $4000{-}500~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ using the KBr disk method. The pore size and volume were determined by ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherms obtained at $77~\mathrm{K}$ on a Quantachrome Autosorb-1 (USA). The sample was outgassed at ${{10}^{-3}}$ Torr and $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for approximately $6\textup{h}$ prior to the adsorption experiment. UV-Vis spectra were recorded by a U-3010 spectrophotometer (Hitachi, Japan). A 510 digital $\mathrm{\pH}$ meter (China) was used to measure the $\mathrm{\pH}$ values of the solutions.
## 3  Results and discussion
## 3.1  Crystal growth of iron based MOFs with different shapes and sizes
Uniform octahedrons can be directly synthesized using $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and terephthalic acid as the standard solution via a facile solvothermal method without any capping agent. The SEM and TEM images reveal that all of the nanoparticles were octahedrons with an average size of $1\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (Figure 1(a–c)). With the introduction of 0.2 equiv. of sodium acetate, spindle-shaped morphology rather than octahedrons resulted (Figure 1(d–f)). The average widths and lengths were 0.7 and $7~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ , respectively. With the increase of the concentration of sodium acetate, the size of the spindleshaped particles gradually decreased from micrometers to nanometers. Specifically, the length of the particles was reduced to $600~\mathrm{{nm}}$ when the added sodium acetate was increased to 1.5 equiv. (Figure 2). If glycerol instead of sodium acetate was added to the standard solution, however, pure phase crystals with bipyramidal hexagonal shape were obtained (Figure $\mathrm{1(g\mathrm{-i}))}$ .
Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns of iron based MOFs with different shapes (Figure 3) showed that spindleshaped morphology exhibited the major peaks of Fe-MIL88, no peaks representing impurity were observed. Octahedrals and bipyramidal hexagonals not only revealed the major peaks of MIL-88, but also weak characteristic peaks of MIL-101 [25], which might be because MIL-88 and MIL-101 co-existed in the reaction system and may be interchangeable under certain conditions. Therefore, octahedral and bipyramidal hexagonal structures were the respective symbiotic phases of the MIL-88 and MIL-101, which indicates that the structure of MOFs is easily affected by adjusting the reagent.
IR spectroscopic measurements (Figure 4) revealed that all shapes exhibited the similar characteristic stretching vibration similar to those of the functional groups of the terephthalic acid ligand. This result confirmed that these particles, although they have different shapes, demonstrate similar coordination patterns. For the estimation of the porosity, typical nitrogen adsorption desorption isotherms at $77~\mathrm{{K}}$ were recorded (Figure 5), and the results show that the octahedron and bipyramidal hexagon have high specific surface areas and certain pore volume. The specific surface areas were measured as $1.157{\times}10^{3}\ \mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ for the octahedral particles and $7.878\times10^{2}~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ for the bipyramidal hexagonal particles. The corresponding pore-size distributions showed the average pore diameter to be $7{-}10\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ for both the octahedron and bipyramidal hexagon. The specific surface area of spindle was only $13.75~\mathrm{m}^{\bar{2}}/\mathrm{g}$ , however, the number of nitrogen adsorption-desorption isotherms was very low. Therefore, the porosity of the spindle was too low to be ignored. To further conform the purity of the iron-based MOFs, elemental analysis was used to investigate the chemical composition of iron-based MOFs. Results showed that all of the shaped MOFs have similar chemical compositions that include iron, carbon and oxygen atoms (Figure S1, Table S1, Supporting Information online). These results indicate that the unreacted reagents and solutions were completely removed.
## 3.2 The formation mechanisms of the iron based MOFs
Understanding the growth behaviour and morphology evolution are crucial for the controllable synthesis of MOFs. In this work, we observed morphology evolution process by analyzing the SEM images of octahedron taken at different crystallization time points. It involves nucleation, aggregation, growth and finally annealing [26–28]. At the early stages of the reaction, quasi-spherical seeds were observed, which had started to agglomerate (Figure 6(a)). In the next stage, the increased aggregates began to agglomerate into sphere-like particles with rough surface topologies (Figure 6(b)). The aggregated nanospheres slowly annealed into single particles with smooth surfaces (Figure 6(c)) and ultimately formed uniform octahedral particles (Figure 6(d)). From the view point of thermodynamics, the crystals tended to grow into the shapes that have the lowest surface free energy. Here, uniform octahedrons are dominant in the final products because they have relatively low surface free energy [29,30].
Carboxylate salts-such as sodium acetate and sodium format have been reported as effective modulators in tailoring the morphology and size of MOFs crystals [31]. In this work, we tried to introduce sodium acetate to manipulate the shape of iron-based MOFs. As shown in Figure 2, with the addition of sodium acetate into the standard solution, spindle-shaped Fe-MIL-88 MOFs rather than octahedrons were obtained. Moreover, the size of spindle-shaped particles gradually decreased with the increasing concentration of sodium acetate. The effect of sodium acetate upon particle size is probably due to its basicity $(\mathrm{p}K_{\mathrm{b}}{=}9.25)$ , which can facilitate the deprotonation of the organic linker $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ . In this case, more deprotonated $\mathrm{\bfH}_{2}\mathrm{\bfBDC}$ ions were available for coordination with metal ions, which resulted in accelerated nucleation rates and decreased crystal size. This proposal can be identified by measuring the $\mathrm{\pH}$ values of the reaction mixtures (Table 1). With the addition of sodium acetate, the $\mathsf{p H}$ of the solution increases, which indicates that more deprotonated $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ are available in the solution. A control experiment was conducted by adding of sodium tetraborate (which is also an alkaline molecule) to the standard solution, Spindle-shaped crystals could be obtained if all the other conditions remained the same (Figure S2), which indicated that the size and morphology of the particles could be tuned by altering the $\mathrm{\pH}$ of the reaction solution and that an alkaline environment is beneficial for the formation of Fe-MIL-88. High nucleation concentration was beneficial for the formation of rods and other elongated structures [32].
When glycerol rather than sodium acetate was added to the standard solution, the bipyramidal hexagonal shape could be obtained. This result first indicates that glycerol can control the growth of bipyramidal hexagons mainly due to its hydroxyl groups. The hydroxyl groups of glycerol can strictly limit the growth of the crystals in separate places [33] and thus improve the uniformity and monodispersity of the particles. Second, hydroxyl groups of glycerol can prevent Oswald ripening during the synthesis process, which leads to higher uniformity among the bipyramidal hexagons [34]. It has known that Oswald ripening often occurs in a kinetically controlled reaction, which occurs between larger and smaller particles [32]. In our case, Oswald ripening occurred when the reaction temperature was decreased from 120 to $90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and two size-regimes of MOFs thus obtained (Figure S3). The larger size was bipyramidal hexagonals of $2~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ and the smaller size was uniform octahedrons of $200\mathrm{nm}$ . Although Oswald ripening is unstoppable, it can be slowed if the solubility or diffusivity is decreased [32]. Because glycerol can decrease the diffusivity of the nucleated solution, it can protect the crystal from ripening and forming uniform bipyramidal hexagons. To further confirm this conclusion, glycol, which has similar chemical structure and properties as glycerol, was used as a control for comparison.
Results similar to the use of glycerol were obtained (Figure S4). These combined results indicate that the crystal nucleation rate and orientational growth play major roles in determining the final shape of iron-based MOFs.
## 3.3  Shape-dependent catalytic properties
Iron-based MOFs such as Fe-MIL- $88\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ [35], MIL-68 and MIL-100 [36] have been reported to exhibit excellent intrinsic peroxidase-like activity, because such activity is closely correlated with the size and morphology of nanoparticles [37]. Therefore, investigation of the shape-dependent peroxidase-like properties of iron-based MOFs is quite useful for their further applications. As Figure 7(A) shows, the peroxidase-like activities of the as-prepared iron-based MOFs have the order of bipyramidal hexagonal>octahedron>spindle to promote the oxidation reaction of the TMB- $\mathbf{\cdotH}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ system. The same order of catalytic activity was also obtained when ABTS was used as the substrate (Figure 7(B)). The timedependent catalytic activities also confirm that the bipyramidal hexagon had higher catalytic activity and efficiency than the octahedron or the spindle (Figure S5).
The bipyramidal hexagon showed the highest catalytic activity because its structure had cracks in the catalytic reaction, whereas the octahedron and spindle retained their framework integrity before and after the peroxidase reaction (Figure 8). The cracked structure of bipyramidal hexagonal greatly enhanced the specific surface areas (more active metal sites were exposed to react with the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and TMB). This result was very interesting because the obtained bipyramidal hexagonal particles were found to be stable in organic solvents; they were only unstable in water. Although the structure of bipyramidal hexagonal had some cracks, its cell cytotoxicity was very low. Even at the highest concentration of $1~\mathrm{mg/mL}$ , more than $85\%$ of the A549 cell remained viable (Figure S6). This high catalytic activity combined with excellent degradability, biocompatibility and high porosity mean that bipyramidal hexagons can be further applied in biocatalysis. Compared to the bipyramidal hexagons, the most important property of the octahedron was its high water stability, which means it can be recyclable after catalytic reactions. Although the catalytic activity of the spindle was lower than the bipyramidal hexagons and octahedron, it also had significant catalytic activity because the surface of the spindle has metal active sites, this means, that the catalytic reaction can occur on the surface of the MOFs. In addition, it could easily reach different sizes, from micron to nanometer, which make it very useful in further study of the size-dependent properties of MOFs. Finally, the possible reason for the cracking of the bipyramidal hexagon is that the bipyramidal hexagon has a better “breathing effect”, which means that it can absorb a great deal of water in its porous surface, which makes its volume expand and leads to cracking [38].
## 4 Conclusions
We have demonstrated a new strategy for good control of the size and morphology of iron-terephthalic acid metal organic frameworks. The crystal structure, morphology, and formation mechanisms were thoroughly studied. The ironbased MOFs exhibited shape-dependent catalytic activity, which means that they can be used in different catalytic fields. For example, the degradability of the bipyramidal hexagon makes it a promising material in biocatalysis. The octahedron exhibits high water stability, so it can be recyclable after catalytic reactions. The size of the spindle can be adjusted from micron to nanometer, so it is very useful in further studies of the size-dependent property of MOFs. These findings may shed new light on the controllable synthesis of metal-organic frameworks as well as their use in catalytic applications.
